I went to the App Store lab that they have there in Apple Park.

217
00:15:03,120 --> 00:15:08,560
I had contact with one of the people from the App Store Connect API luckily.

218
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:12,160
And she said, Oh, you need to talk to this person to get it sorted.

219
00:15:12,640 --> 00:15:14,080
And then talked to her.

220
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:15,280
She wrote down a number.

221
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:19,520
And then she listened and looked at the screen, typed the number, wrote something down.

222
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:21,440
And like, Oh, gosh, she didn't say a word.

223
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:24,080
And I was like, Give me something.

224
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:35,120
And then, yeah, it takes a long time because it's currently stuck in legal because they wanted to make sure we didn't sell their product.

225
00:15:35,520 --> 00:15:38,400
We didn't sell App Store Connect as a product necessarily.

226
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:42,960
We actually did extra things and not just literally be App Store Connect.

227
00:15:43,280 --> 00:15:45,840
The name was okay because we have four App Store Connect.

228
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:50,800
And as long as you say it's four or something instead of trying to be that something, it should be okay.

229
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:57,040
And then I think the next morning, yeah, the day after.theC, we got approved.

230
00:15:57,600 --> 00:15:59,520
Do you think it was actually going to go through anyway?

231
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:00,800
And then it's like, wow.

232
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:03,200
It may have just been in a giant pile.

233
00:16:03,600 --> 00:16:06,080
Well, they will never say so, but it definitely helped, of course.

234
00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:15,280
That's why WDC is such an important week in general, I think, to go to the labs and talk to engineers and talk to people, talk to Apple.

235
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:19,280
This is the one moment of the year where you can actually talk to these people, which is very powerful.

236
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:22,080
What is it, Developer Support Ticket, is that what it's called?

237
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:24,880
Where like Apple developers get two of them a year where you can talk.

238
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:27,200
The idea is that you can talk to an engineer.

239
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:33,440
Is there a mechanism for that for like app review or app store questions?

240
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:35,440
I don't know.

241
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:36,640
I've never tried it actually.

242
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:37,440
I've never used it.

243
00:16:37,760 --> 00:16:38,240
Yeah, I have no idea.

244
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:44,320
The stereotype with those is that you only get to a year so developers never use any because they're afraid to waste it.

245
00:16:45,440 --> 00:16:49,200
And that's definitely why I'd use one next week just to see how they are, how they do it.

246
00:16:49,440 --> 00:16:58,240
Because so much of the time it's like just getting a human being inside the spaceship to recognize it and then flag it somewhere, like because they can like Slack or email someone.

247
00:16:58,560 --> 00:17:03,760
But I wonder if like, if you ever get stuck in one of those, if you could use one of those to get to get hold of a person.

248
00:17:04,080 --> 00:17:05,600
Because it just kind of worked for you all that.

249
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:05,920
Yeah.

250
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:08,000
WWDC happened to be coming up.

251
00:17:08,240 --> 00:17:11,120
But if it had been August, like, what would you have done?

252
00:17:11,600 --> 00:18:01,280
I've never used one of them, but it was my first time at WWDC this year and I managed to go to the Upstore Connect API lab and I had about ten, twelve questions and they were incredibly kind to just let me stay after my session as well and I basically went through all of the feedback it was literally like thirty, forty minutes with one person was like a lot better than just me filing feedback for about and the feedback was important as well because then I could go and say like look, I filed this radar this is all of like all of the context and I basically got answers for absolutely everything so yeah it was honestly invaluable just for that yeah it's crazy how useful I mean I guess the this sounds really obvious on the surface, but talking to the actual people instead of just filing into a giant, you know, anonymous bucket, it can make a really big difference.

253
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:03,200
So you got it, it was approved.

254
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:12,320
I assume in May you had all these plans for exciting launches and especially knowing you all, which we should get into at some point, you do crazy things.

255
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:14,320
And so I assume you were very excited about it in May.

256
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:18,240
And then by the time it got to June, it was like, it's just, it's out there now.

257
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:20,960
And like, how did that launch itself go at that point?

258
00:18:21,680 --> 00:18:23,600
I think it's very much like every other launch.

259
00:18:23,760 --> 00:18:27,120
It's like, it feels so anticlimax, like an anticlimax.

260
00:18:27,360 --> 00:18:29,440
It's like, okay, I guess this is a thing now.

261
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:37,840
Yeah, and then slowly but steadily, the first users started dripping in.

262
00:18:38,080 --> 00:18:40,480
It went quite fast, quite quickly.

263
00:18:40,800 --> 00:18:44,560
Obviously helped that we both do a lot of public speaking and a lot of conferences.

264
00:18:45,760 --> 00:18:50,160
So we always have like a room full of potential users in front of us.

265
00:18:50,960 --> 00:19:00,000
But I think what we noticed quicker than anything before is how much the app has been promoted by our users, not even by ourselves.

266
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:06,400
Like every time someone mentioned App Store Connect on Twitter, people would just tag Helm and say, just.

267
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:07,680
I just use Helm instead.

268
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,400
And that's the best feeling you can have, I think.

269
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:12,160
From the beginning as well.

270
00:19:12,320 --> 00:19:14,240
Like that was pretty impressive.

271
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:21,040
Like it was just like, yeah, we didn't really have to do obviously we've done a lot, but we didn't have to do a lot to kind of like just promote it.

272
00:19:21,520 --> 00:19:30,640
And I think it probably has to do with it's probably the first time that we have an app where the target audience is the same as the audience we've got online anyway.

273
00:19:30,880 --> 00:19:35,200
Like every time we go to conferences, that's the exact audience we want to be hitting.

274
00:19:35,840 --> 00:19:38,320
So yeah, I think that's probably part of it.

275
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:49,200
But yeah, it's been surprising that like every week you wake up one day and it's like someone talking about like Helm, or someone complains about App Store Connect, and then in the comments it's like, oh, just go use Helm.

276
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:52,080
And it's like, oh, we didn't even have to jump on that thread.

277
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:58,000
Someone else did, like, just set it for us, which is which is worth ten times more than somebody promoting it for yourself.

278
00:19:58,240 --> 00:19:58,880
It's a great sign.

279
00:19:59,520 --> 00:20:03,680
So what other marketing things have you done?

280
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:09,920
So obviously, you know, you go to conferences, maybe you make some stickers or pins or something.

281
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:11,760
But is there anything unique?

282
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:15,040
I'm laughing, and Hit is already, he's pulling something out here.

283
00:20:16,560 --> 00:20:19,920
So for the audience that's just listening, Hit is holding it up.

284
00:20:21,600 --> 00:20:25,600
What we in America would call a jersey, what I believe everywhere else in the world would call a kit.

285
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:30,720
Is a kit the whole thing or is that also just the top part?

286
00:20:30,880 --> 00:20:30,960
Yeah.

287
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:32,080
What's the top part called?

288
00:20:32,560 --> 00:20:34,880
In the UK they have like a thousand words for it here.

289
00:20:35,040 --> 00:20:40,720
So I think some people call it top, some people call it kit as well, like just the top bit, I don't know.

290
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:41,920
Just call it whatever.

291
00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:49,920
Anyway, it's a football or again, as Americans would call it, soccer shirt thing that you'd wear while playing the sport.

292
00:20:50,160 --> 00:20:51,840
And it has a big Helm logo on the front.

293
00:20:52,240 --> 00:20:54,880
As we launched Helm, it took off quite a bit.

294
00:20:55,200 --> 00:20:56,880
And Paul was visiting Amsterdam.

295
00:20:57,360 --> 00:20:59,760
I think for a conference or maybe just visiting, I don't know.

296
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:05,360
And then we were like, oh, we need to do something fun marketing wise, like do something really crazy.

297
00:21:06,080 --> 00:21:13,600
And then Paul was collecting football shirts and it's like, oh, we could sponsor, maybe we could sponsor like a really low tier.

298
00:21:14,160 --> 00:21:17,600
And as soon as he sent it, I said, You don't have to tell me more.

299
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:26,480
Because I knew in Amsterdam there's an amateur football club called AFFA Zwift, which means Amsterdam Football Association Zwift.

300
00:21:27,040 --> 00:21:32,000
So the name, the football, and it was also in white and blue, so it's like on brand.

301
00:21:32,480 --> 00:21:33,120
It's your colors.

302
00:21:33,360 --> 00:21:34,320
It has the word Zwift in it.

303
00:21:34,720 --> 00:21:34,800
it.

304
00:21:35,200 --> 00:21:43,920
And is there any place where you can find an audience of IOS developers that is more obvious than a low tier football club in IS?

305
00:21:44,080 --> 00:21:45,760
I'm pretty sure we have no downloads from that.

306
00:21:46,160 --> 00:21:46,800
Zero, I think.

307
00:21:47,120 --> 00:21:49,440
Probably web visits, but no downloads.

308
00:21:49,840 --> 00:21:58,000
Actually, I had someone on the conference say to me, like, oh yeah, I didn't know Helm, but when I heard the story about the jerseys, I went to download it.

309
00:21:59,040 --> 00:21:59,280
Nice.

310
00:22:01,440 --> 00:22:06,960
But I think, like I said before, I think I'm pretty sure the team itself thinks we're a boating company.

311
00:22:07,520 --> 00:22:11,200
You heard him say this, you're like, oh, we got to sponsor this team.

312
00:22:11,680 --> 00:22:11,760
Yeah.

313
00:22:12,160 --> 00:22:12,560
What then?

314
00:22:13,280 --> 00:22:14,000
Did you call them up?

315
00:22:14,160 --> 00:22:15,360
Yeah, no, just send an email.

316
00:22:15,760 --> 00:22:17,680
Hey, I want to sponsor one of your teams.

317
00:22:18,080 --> 00:22:19,120
And it's like, okay, great.

318
00:22:19,440 --> 00:22:19,920
Which teams?

319
00:22:20,160 --> 00:22:21,760
We have this, we have that, we have this.

320
00:22:21,920 --> 00:22:25,120
It's like, well, the lowest one will have Swift.

321
00:22:26,880 --> 00:22:32,320
We'll have Swift six, I take, because it was also a point with Swift language that was coming out.

322
00:22:32,560 --> 00:22:33,520
I think it was Swift six.

323
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:34,800
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

324
00:22:35,760 --> 00:22:37,360
So it's like, yeah, let's do that one.

325
00:22:37,840 --> 00:22:40,800
And then the cost was actually quite okay, was not as high.

326
00:22:41,280 --> 00:22:47,840
So if you're listening, great marketing tip, sponsor, local football club or team clubs.

327
00:22:48,960 --> 00:22:59,440
I will say, like, obviously, this is not a user acquisition strategy, but it does add to the sort of joy and fun around the brand.

328
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:10,560
And like, I, from the outside, Helm is an app that has, I wouldn't necessarily call it a cult following, but it has a dedicated set of users who just love it.

329
00:23:10,720 --> 00:23:14,000
It makes their lives easier, but also it's like, it's fun to be part of the club.

330
00:23:14,320 --> 00:23:20,480
And I think that's why you get so much of the other people responding to other people saying, hey, you should use Helm.

331
00:23:20,560 --> 00:23:23,520
Because it's like, yes, they're saying it because it's a tool they like and enjoy.

332
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:25,840
But also it's like, heyy, you should join our club.

333
00:23:25,920 --> 00:23:28,080
This is a fun thing that I'm also, you know, kind of part of.

334
00:23:28,560 --> 00:23:42,240
And so, I don't know, this is like really maybe stretching it, but I do think this is a thing that you can do that if you're just vibe coding and playing the MRR maximization game, you're never going to do something like this.

335
00:23:42,720 --> 00:23:59,360
But as an indie who's just making something kind of joyful and high craft and trying to build a loyal user base, I think doing fun stuff like this, one, it just makes your life more joyful, but also I think it does have some benefits to kind of the brand.

336
00:23:59,840 --> 00:24:03,840
You said the app kind able to sustain you?

337
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:08,080
Well, not fully because it's also where we're the two of us, so we're splitting it.

338
00:24:08,240 --> 00:24:13,760
Although most of it we just reinvest in marketing things or into other small things like that.

339
00:24:14,080 --> 00:24:14,640
What does that mean?

340
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:15,920
Reinvest in the marketing things.

341
00:24:16,160 --> 00:24:18,800
Is this this is just more teams like to get swag?

342
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:19,360
Yes.

343
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:30,560
Also, open AI credits just in advance just to make sure that those features because the AI features are the strong, the most important features in our app.

344
00:24:30,880 --> 00:24:35,440
So we just want to make sure that there's enough credits on there to last a long time.

345
00:24:36,960 --> 00:24:46,560
Also, we have now we have another person helping us out because as we joined RevenueCat a year ago, we realized we would get less time, but that's difficult.

346
00:24:46,880 --> 00:24:48,240
We'll catch on to that story later.

347
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:57,200
But then we're like, oh, let's ask another person that we trust and know well to come join the ship.

348
00:24:59,360 --> 00:25:06,960
Okay, so it's very much in the like this is a product in your lineup category, even if it's kind of the bigger one?

349
00:25:07,760 --> 00:25:09,360
Yeah, yeah, for now it is.

350
00:25:09,920 --> 00:25:11,760
We love to make this our full time thing.

351
00:25:12,320 --> 00:25:16,080
We think we can get there within a few years at least because it's still.

352
00:25:16,560 --> 00:25:18,400
growing every month quite a lot.

353
00:25:18,720 --> 00:25:20,400
Because that's the thing with these types of tools, right?

354
00:25:20,640 --> 00:25:23,680
Like, the more people use it, the faster you grow.

355
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:29,680
As long as it's a very stable line, it's not a spike, but it's just a very stable linear line almost.

356
00:25:30,320 --> 00:25:46,400
Well, and I would imagine, given the user base, that once somebody decides they're going to use it, because it's a tool and it becomes part of your, how you work, I imagine your churn is actually relatively low compared to, like, a normal consumer app.

357
00:25:47,840 --> 00:25:48,880
Has that borne out?

358
00:25:49,600 --> 00:25:50,240
I believe so.

359
00:25:50,560 --> 00:25:50,640
Yeah.

360
00:25:51,040 --> 00:25:55,120
I mean, I have to say, I don't keep an eye on this too much, to be honest.

361
00:25:55,280 --> 00:26:11,680
We can say like the growth has been steady and I know like over this this year we've had like less time as well and there are some like very complex features that we've been working on as well that we believe like are going to make a difference and we get a lot of feedback for features that we don't have as well.

362
00:26:12,400 --> 00:26:18,160
And so we kind of like get a sense of what people want and what we can do to kind of like grow in this sense.

363
00:26:18,800 --> 00:26:23,680
So at least from my side, I'm not super obsessed with like the the analytics of things.

364
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:28,560
I'm more obsessed with kind of just getting value to to our users.

365
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:29,840
And sometimes that's quite.

366
00:26:30,480 --> 00:26:34,880
complicated because of the nature of how it is and all the edge cases that we have.

367
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:40,880
But I try to focus more on that than just trying to look at numbers and stuff.

368
00:26:41,600 --> 00:26:48,400
But yeah, we have a very clear vision of what we want, what we want to get and what we have to do to get there.

369
00:26:48,800 --> 00:26:49,840
That's where our focus is.

370
00:26:50,320 --> 00:26:51,760
Okay, let's pull on that thread then.

371
00:26:51,840 --> 00:26:56,080
Because like, I think we've established the main marketing channel here is.

372
00:26:56,800 --> 00:27:09,600
product and goofy swag more than you know you're not running influencer ads or anything like that which for developer tool probably is the right call we're now at like a year and a half that it's been in the store, right?

373
00:27:10,400 --> 00:27:10,880
Give or take.

374
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:21,040
So from that starting point, the initial version of the app, what are kind of the big features that you've done that have kind of moved the needle a little bit?

375
00:27:21,360 --> 00:27:26,160
Obviously, the biggest one is translations, but we basically had that from day one.

376
00:27:26,560 --> 00:27:36,400
So our goal is that anywhere in the app or anywhere that you actually have localized content, you are able to translate it very, very easily with one click.

377
00:27:37,280 --> 00:27:49,680
So basically, this is if you're writing release notes and you're shipping to multiple stores or you're writing keywords and you want to translate them to all of your localizations and all of your product pages in all of the countries that you support.

378
00:27:50,640 --> 00:27:57,280
You can basically do it with one click and you don't have to actually hire a service or anything like that to do that.

379
00:27:57,440 --> 00:27:59,280
We use AI to do it.

380
00:27:59,840 --> 00:28:06,400
You can use your own API key, but we kind of like just provide support out of the box without having to do anything.

381
00:28:06,640 --> 00:28:10,720
It's just as easy as you can get just one tap and then you translate the content.

382
00:28:10,960 --> 00:28:15,760
So you've got a better chance of people actually downloading your app in those markets.

383
00:28:16,400 --> 00:28:18,240
You said this is obviously the biggest thing.

384
00:28:18,560 --> 00:28:28,080
So like based on what you're hearing from people, this is is the biggest, like, if I lost this app today, this is what would make my work harder or take longer or whatever.

385
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:43,120
This is also the main reason why we still don't have all App Store Connect features in there today is because we really, really tried to look at each part of App Store Connect and think what is currently the bottleneck in this workflow.

386
00:28:43,360 --> 00:28:47,840
Like, how can we make you do the same with as little effort as possible?

387
00:28:48,240 --> 00:28:50,960
Because in the end, we're all just lazy people.

388
00:28:51,360 --> 00:28:52,320
Like everyone's lazy.

389
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:55,040
Everyone likes to do as little as possible and as fast as possible.

390
00:28:56,160 --> 00:28:57,120
Hence the AOL AI.

391
00:28:59,200 --> 00:29:00,000
That's normal, right?

392
00:29:00,320 --> 00:29:07,520
And I feel like, well, with everyone launching their app, like actually putting it on the App Store should not feel as painful as it was.

393
00:29:07,920 --> 00:29:10,480
And I think that's how we tackle every feature.

394
00:29:10,720 --> 00:29:17,360
Like just look at it from the with our developers' eyes and see, like, okay, how can we make this part better?

395
00:29:17,680 --> 00:29:29,840
And one of them is like every single update from Helm is shipped with Helm as well, which is very meta, but it's also make sure that we stay on top of things.

396
00:29:30,800 --> 00:29:34,800
One of the reasons why we got app clips aboard.

397
00:29:34,800 --> 00:29:35,760
App Clips support quite early.

398
00:29:36,320 --> 00:29:42,240
Well, so little people use App Clips is because the iOS app of Helm has App Clips, and otherwise we couldn't ship Helm with Helm.

399
00:29:44,560 --> 00:29:47,600
So that's why we added App Clips support so early already.

400
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:55,840
So speaking of the iOS app, that was the big story when we did our live episode, because you were basically launching it that week.

401
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:59,600
Like on your one year anniversary, you were kind of launching the iOS version.

402
00:30:00,160 --> 00:30:04,160
My memory is that the iOS version was a highly requested feature.

403
00:30:04,960 --> 00:30:39,440
But you didn't just take Helm and make it where it works on iOS, you added some stuff that was kind of mobile specific can you talk about like you know that process of like why you decided to make an iOS app and then what you actually did yeah I think as you said it was it was highly requested and I think like a lot of people were saying we even had like someone come up at the conference and wanting like a watch iOS app so that they could just ship their app with one button on the watch which is still probably somewhere in in the roadmap but But yeah, I think people want to do stuff on the go.

404
00:30:39,760 --> 00:30:52,400
So for example, if you upload a build and you don't want to, you're about to leave like the house and you just want to submit for review while you're waiting for that to process.

405
00:30:52,560 --> 00:30:53,600
You don't have to stay at home.

406
00:30:53,760 --> 00:30:54,480
You can do it on the go.

407
00:30:55,040 --> 00:30:56,560
So that was the whole plan.

408
00:30:57,040 --> 00:31:00,480
So we just wanted to do features that made sense on mobile.

409
00:31:01,200 --> 00:31:07,520
We didn't want to bring everything one-for-one to the iOS app and just do things that make sense while you're on the go.

410
00:31:08,480 --> 00:31:16,240
So you can view your screenshots, you can actually submit for review, edit your release notes, translate all that kind of stuff.

411
00:31:16,720 --> 00:31:21,520
So that was, yeah, we also brought a bit of fun to the app as well, as always.

412
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:25,680
We had a like iOS-only feature as well called POM Passport.

413
00:31:26,560 --> 00:31:41,280
because we were planning to launch it during WWC and for everyone who has been to WWC, you know, you get you talk to a lot of developers, but I don't know, I always find it hard to remember names, but I always remember which apps they worked on.

